hi there uh day 68. it was a beautiful  day i've uh walked the dog in the sun  again in the forest  uh i want to talk about time time and  language learning  time is ultimately the  rarest resource that we have  in life i mean it's the ultimate  non-renewable resource we can't make it  we can make products we can plant stuff  in the ground and it'll grow  but we can't make time  so  uh when it comes to language learning we  have a limited amount of time available  because we have other activities  and so we have to make decisions like  today  it's a lovely day my wife went to play  golf  so i was tempted to play golf  but then i would get  no uh korean studying done  we're going out to dinner tonight  so if it weren't for the 90-day  challenge i probably would have gone to  play golf  in the end i studied korean  and then i also walked the dog for a  good hour and a half  uh  which was great i loved it  so in the end i was thinking of this you  know if i'd gone to play golf i would  have enjoyed my golf  and that's all i would have gotten out  of it i would have got the  whatever my wife played nine holes so  you know two and a half hours or so of  enjoyment  minus the time driving there and back  uh  and when i drive there with my wife i  can't listen to korean  uh so instead i did probably three hours  here at home between linking and reading  on my ipad  and then i had another hour and a half  in the bush while listening  and walking the dog  so and that was enjoyable maybe not as  enjoyable as playing golf  but enjoyable nevertheless however you  know when you look at time you can just  think about this you know there are  those activities  which basically the enjoyment or lack of  enjoyment is basically all contained in  the activity  i guess playing golf there's some  benefit in that you're out in the fresh  air then there are those activities that  may or not may or may not be enjoyable  but they have  an effect an impact a result a  consequence that consequence may be good  or bad so i you know i think you know  anytime you have a decision on how to  spend your time  if you choose to spend your time getting  drunk  you know that might be enjoyable but it  might be have might have some rather  unpleasant consequences  uh  if you like in my own case i find  studying flashcards not all that  enjoyable  i might do it while i'm waiting you know  or for my food in a restaurant or  something  i wouldn't spend an hour or an hour and  a half doing flashcards because it's not  enjoyable but it probably has some  benefit i'm not sure of that but i'm  sure it has some benefit i should say it  does have some benefit i'm not sure how  much benefit it has on the other hand  i'm walking the dog which is good for  the dog and also  gets me some  brownie points with my son and his  family and  my wife's mother my  son's mother-in-law  so there's some benefit there i think  anytime you're you're doing something  for someone else  because we operate on the basis of  reciprocity you feel good about doing it  and you're contributing towards a  reciprocal  favor benefit that those other people  are going to bestow back are you  so the dog's benefiting  my wife's mother-in-law's her mother my  son's mother-in-law is happy  uh i'm getting exercise and i'm  listening to korean  wow and by listening to korean  i know from experience you know how much  enjoyment i get from  watching a movie and  as we all do watching a movie in a  foreign language  my wife is watching curling on tv it  doesn't matter whether it's the russian  girls uh talking to each other screaming  you know  uh or you know japanese korean i don't  quite get it yet but i'll get some words  um you know they're swiss gals i don't  if they're into s  swiss german i don't understand it but  um  so that you get all of this benefit so  all i'm saying is  it's difficult to find the time  to study  a language uh  i prefer to find things to do that i  find enjoyable and that i know are going  to give me benefit such as my focus on  listening and reading  and  when faced with the decision as to  whether i should  do the sort of studying activity  or do something which is not related to  studying  um it's always a close call but certain  um certain decisions are made for me i  play my hockey three times a week  but the optional ones it's often a  toss-up so  sometimes i'll say oh well i'll go and  play golf although it may that may not  be  high on my agenda makes my wife happy so  i get some reciprocal benefit there so  anyway just philosophizing about  time  and how  you know we can't stretch time  but we can get more benefit out of our  use of time if our activity is both  beneficial to us possibly beneficial to  others and has other long-term  benefits that are going to  bring benefit later on ultimately what  we want is to maximize happiness not  just the immediate short-term happiness  but sort of  a constant state  of happiness  a because of what we choose to do at a  given point in time and b because of  what we invest in  activities health  knowledge  uh helping friends etc  being a good citizen like picking up my  dog's poo when i go and walk the dog  in the expectation that others will do  the same  so that we're living in a society where  people are responsible towards each  other and that makes all of our lives  more pleasant and i think that's a large  part of why we contribute to these  various social programs that help people  who are less lucky  because a lot of us who are better off  have just been lucky in most cases  so anyway i thought i'd philosophize  well beyond the issue of time but time  is the ultimate  scarce rare commodity when it comes to  language learning  so we all have to make decisions as to  how we  find the time and how we spend the time  in our language learning i hope all that  makes sense the more i talk the less  sense it makes to me  bye for now
 

